.TH GMESG 1 "September 2026" "gmesg" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gmesg \- a terminal client for Google Messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gmesg
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR phone ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.br
.B gmesg pair
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B gmesg daemon
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gmesg
is a terminal client for Google Messages (RCS/SMS), built on
.B libgm
\(em the protocol library behind the mautrix-gmessages Matrix bridge \(em
which communicates directly with Google's pairing and messaging protocol,
without browser automation.
.PP
There are three ways to run it:
.IP \(bu 2
.B gmesg pair
links your phone once, via a QR code rendered in the terminal.
.IP \(bu 2
.B gmesg daemon
runs in the background, holding the connection to Google's servers open
and firing a desktop notification for every incoming message.
.IP \(bu 2
.B gmesg
with no subcommand opens the interactive TUI, or, given
.BR \-p / \-m / \-f ,
sends a single message and exits without opening the TUI at all.
.PP
The TUI does not talk to Google directly \(em it connects to
.B gmesg daemon
over a Unix domain socket, so the daemon and the TUI (and any scripted
.B \-p / \-m
sends) all share one live connection and one pairing. Start the daemon
before the TUI:
.PP
.RS 4
gmesg daemon &
.br
gmesg
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B pair
Display a QR code in the terminal. Scan it from the phone being paired,
via Google Messages > Settings > Device pairing > Pair a device. The code
refreshes automatically every 30 seconds until scanned. If a session
already exists for the selected profile, pairing again replaces it.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show debug-level protocol logs while pairing.
.RE
.TP
.B daemon
Connect and stay connected in the background. Runs
.B notify-send
for every incoming message (sender and text, or an attachment summary
when the message has no text), and serves the Unix socket the TUI and
one-shot sends connect through. Intended to be started from a
login/autostart script, in the same manner as
.BR dunst (1).
Terminates on SIGINT or SIGTERM (e.g. Ctrl-C), saving the session before
exit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
Paired identity to use. Defaults to the plain, un-namespaced session.
Running the TUI and the daemon concurrently against the same profile is
safe: they share a single connection through the socket. Two
.B daemon
instances against the
.I same
profile, however, contend for one long-poll connection to Google's
servers and can corrupt the saved session. Assign each independent
pairing its own
.B \-\-profile
name.
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-phone " numbers"
Recipient phone number(s) for a one-shot send, comma-separated for a
group. Requires the daemon to already be running.
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-message " text"
Message text to send. Optional if at least one
.B \-f
is given.
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-file " path"
Attach a file (photo, gif, video, ...) to a one-shot send. Repeatable for
multiple attachments.
.SH INTERACTIVE TUI
The TUI has two panes: a conversation list and a message thread, plus a
vim-style modal thread view.
.SS Conversation list
.TP
.B j / k / down / up
Move the selection.
.TP
.B gg / G
Jump to the top / bottom of the list.
.TP
.B /
Filter the list (built-in fuzzy filter).
.TP
.B enter
Open the selected conversation.
.TP
.B n
Start a new conversation (prompts for a recipient).
.TP
.B q
Quit gmesg.
.SS Thread \(em normal mode
.TP
.B j / k / down / up
Scroll one line.
.TP
.B ctrl-d / ctrl-u
Scroll half a page down / up.
.TP
.B gg / G
Jump to the top / bottom of the thread.
.TP
.B J / K
Move the message cursor to the next / previous message.
.TP
.B i / a
Enter insert mode to compose a reply.
.TP
.B o
Open the selected message's first link or attachment with the system
default handler.
.TP
.B p
Toggle an inline image preview of the selected message (requires
.BR ueberzug (1)).
.TP
.B r
Open the reaction picker on the selected message.
.TP
.B yy
Yank the selected message's full text to the clipboard.
.TP
.B yc
Yank just the message's one-time passcode (identified among surrounding
digits by proximity to a keyword such as "code" or "OTP" when the message
contains more than one number).
.TP
.B yl
Yank the message's first link.
.TP
.B q / esc
Back to the conversation list.
.PP
Yanking prefers a native clipboard utility
.RB ( xclip / xsel / wl\-copy / pbcopy ),
whichever is found first. Without one \(em for example, when connected
over SSH to a machine with no display \(em it falls back to an OSC52
escape sequence, which still reaches a local clipboard over SSH or inside
tmux/screen with passthrough enabled.
.SS Thread \(em insert mode
.TP
.B enter
Send the composed message (and any pending attachments).
.TP
.B ctrl-t
Attach a file: prompts for a path, with tab-completion.
.TP
.B esc
Back to normal mode.
.SS Reaction picker
.TP
.B h / l / left / right
Move the selection among the eight reaction emoji.
.TP
.B 1\-8
Jump directly to a reaction by position.
.TP
.B enter
Send the selected reaction.
.TP
.B esc
Cancel.
.SS Command mode
Press
.B :
from normal mode to open a command line, with tab-completion over
command names. Equivalent to the key bindings above:
.PP
.RS 4
:q · :back · :open · :react · :preview · :attach · :new · :search · :send · :help
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Overrides the base directory gmesg stores session, cache and socket data
under. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share .
.SH FILES
Paths below are relative to
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/gmesg ,
or
.I ~/.local/share/gmesg
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset; a non-default
.B \-\-profile
namespaces everything under
.IR profiles/ name / .
.TP
.I session.json
The paired identity: auth tokens, device identity, and the keys
negotiated during pairing. Treat it as a credential.
.TP
.I gmesg.sock
Unix domain socket the daemon listens on and the TUI (and one-shot
sends) connect through.
.TP
.I cache/
Downloaded attachments, cached so external viewers such as xdg-open have
a real file path to open.
.SH EXAMPLES
Pair once, then start the daemon and the TUI:
.PP
.RS 4
gmesg pair
.br
gmesg daemon &
.br
gmesg
.RE
.PP
Send a one-shot message from a script:
.PP
.RS 4
gmesg \-p 5551234567 \-m "Deployment complete."
.RE
.PP
Send a photograph with a caption to a group:
.PP
.RS 4
gmesg \-p 5551234567,5557654321 \-m "Status report attached." \-f ~/Pictures/status.jpg
.RE
.PP
Run a second, independent pairing (e.g. a work number) alongside the
default one:
.PP
.RS 4
gmesg \-\-profile work pair
.br
gmesg \-\-profile work daemon &
.br
gmesg \-\-profile work
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR notify\-send (1),
.BR ueberzug (1)
